(>> Solution to Question 1:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Explain the optical waveguiding mechanism of total internal reflection \(TIR\) in optical fibers. State the conditions required for TIR and derive the expression for the critical angle.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Physical Waveguiding Mechanism:) Tj
T*
(   - An optical fiber is a cylindrical dielectric waveguide consisting of an inner glass core) Tj
T*
(   \(refractive index n1\) surrounded by an outer glass cladding \(refractive index n2\).) Tj
T*
(   - Optical signals are guided along the longitudinal axis of the fiber via repetitive Total) Tj
T*
(   Internal Reflection \(TIR\) at the core-cladding boundary, preventing light leakage into the) Tj
T*
(   surrounding environment.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Necessary Conditions for Total Internal Reflection:) Tj
T*
(   - \(a\) Index Condition: The core must be optically denser than the cladding: n1 > n2.) Tj
T*
(   - \(b\) Angular Condition: The angle of incidence phi at the core-cladding interface must exceed) Tj
T*
(   the critical angle: phi > theta_c.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   3. Mathematical Derivation of Critical Angle theta_c:) Tj
T*
(   - Consider a light ray inside the core \(index n1\) incident on the boundary with cladding \(index) Tj
T*
(   n2\) at angle phi.) Tj
T*
(   - By Snell's Law of Refraction: n1 * sin\(phi\) = n2 * sin\(theta_r\), where theta_r is the angle) Tj
T*
(   of refraction in the cladding.) Tj
T*
(   - As the angle of incidence phi increases, the refracted angle theta_r increases.) Tj
T*
(   - The Critical Angle theta_c is defined as the specific angle of incidence for which the angle) Tj
T*
(   of refraction reaches exactly 90 degrees \(theta_r = pi/2\):) Tj
T*
(   n1 * sin\(theta_c\) = n2 * sin\(90 deg\) = n2 * 1.) Tj
T*
(   - Solving for theta_c:) Tj
T*
(   sin\(theta_c\) = n2 / n1 => theta_c = arcsin\( n2 / n1 \).) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   4. Behavior for Different Incident Angles:) Tj
T*
(   - If phi < theta_c: Ordinary refraction occurs; light partially escapes into the cladding) Tj
T*
(   \(refraction loss\).) Tj
T*
(   - If phi = theta_c: The refracted ray grazes along the core-cladding interface.) Tj
T*
(   - If phi > theta_c: 100% of incident optical energy is totally reflected back into the core) Tj
T*
(   with zero transmitted flux, establishing lossless optical waveguiding.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F2 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(>> Solution to Question 2:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Derive from fundamental geometric optics the expressions for the Acceptance Angle and Numerical Aperture \(NA\) of a step-index optical fiber.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Ray Path Geometry at Fiber Entrance:) Tj
T*
(   - Consider a meridional light ray launched from an external medium \(air, index n0 ~= 1.0\) into) Tj
T*
(   the flat entrance face of an optical fiber at angle theta_0 with the fiber axis.) Tj
T*
(   - The ray refracts into the core \(index n1\) at angle theta_1 with the axis.) Tj
T*
(   - By Snell's law at the entrance face:) Tj
T*
(   n0 * sin\(theta_0\) = n1 * sin\(theta_1\).) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Ray Path at the Core-Cladding Boundary:) Tj
T*
(   - The refracted ray propagates and strikes the core-cladding interface at angle of incidence) Tj
T*
(   phi.) Tj
T*
(   - In the right-angled triangle formed by the fiber axis, the entrance face normal, and the ray) Tj
T*
(   path:) Tj
T*
ET

