(>> Solution to Question 4:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Define the normalized frequency parameter \(V-number\) of an optical fiber. Derive the single-mode cutoff condition and obtain the expressions for the number of guided modes in step-index and graded-index fibers.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Definition of Normalized Frequency \(V-Number\):) Tj
T*
(   - The V-number is a dimensionless parameter derived from Maxwell's electromagnetic wave) Tj
T*
(   equations that combines the physical core radius a, operating wavelength lambda, and Numerical) Tj
T*
(   Aperture NA:) Tj
T*
(   V = \(2 * pi * a / lambda\) * NA = \(2 * pi * a / lambda\) * sqrt\( n1^2 - n2^2 \) = \(2 * pi * a /) Tj
T*
(   lambda\) * n1 * sqrt\(2 * Delta\).) Tj
T*
(   - It governs the number of bound electromagnetic modes that can propagate within the dielectric) Tj
T*
(   waveguide.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Single-Mode Cutoff Condition:) Tj
T*
(   - In electromagnetic waveguide theory, the propagation constants beta of transverse modes are) Tj
T*
(   determined by the roots of Bessel functions.) Tj
T*
(   - The fundamental mode \(HE11 or LP01\) has no cutoff frequency \(it propagates for all V > 0\).) Tj
T*
(   - The second higher-order mode \(TE01 / TM01 / HE21, grouped as LP11\) cuts off at the first zero) Tj
T*
(   of the Bessel function J0\(x\), which occurs at:) Tj
T*
(   V_cutoff = 2.40483 \(~ 2.405\).) Tj
T*
(   - Therefore, for an optical fiber to operate strictly in single-mode regime:) Tj
T*
(   V <= 2.405 => lambda >= \(2 * pi * a * NA\) / 2.405 \(Cutoff Wavelength lambda_c\).) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   3. Mode Volume in Step-Index \(SI\) Multi-Mode Fibers:) Tj
T*
(   - When V >> 2.405, a large number of modes propagate.) Tj
T*
(   - Using the WKB approximation and phase-space volume integration over the core cross-section:) Tj
T*
(   M_SI ~= V^2 / 2.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   4. Mode Volume in Parabolic Graded-Index \(GI\) Fibers:) Tj
T*
(   - For an alpha-profile fiber: M_GI ~= \(alpha / \(alpha + 2\)\) * \(V^2 / 2\).) Tj
T*
(   - For a standard parabolic profile where alpha = 2:) Tj
T*
(   M_GI ~= \(2 / 4\) * \(V^2 / 2\) = V^2 / 4.) Tj
T*
(   - A parabolic graded-index fiber guides exactly half as many modes as a step-index fiber with) Tj
T*
(   identical core radius and numerical aperture.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F2 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(>> Solution to Question 5:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Explain all loss mechanisms in optical fibers: intrinsic and extrinsic absorption, Rayleigh scattering, macrobending, and microbending losses. State how modern low-loss fibers achieve 0.2 dB/km attenuation at 1550 nm.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Material Absorption Losses:) Tj
T*
(   - \(a\) Intrinsic Absorption: Inherent properties of pure fused silica \(SiO2\):) Tj
T*
(   * Ultraviolet electronic band-edge absorption \(tails extend up to ~ 800 nm\).) Tj
T*
(   * Infrared vibrational absorption: Molecular fundamental resonances of Si-O bonds at 9.1) Tj
T*
(   microns produce absorption tails above 1600 nm.) Tj
T*
(   - \(b\) Extrinsic Absorption: Caused by microscopic trace impurities:) Tj
T*
(   * Transition metal ions \(Fe, Cu, Cr, Ni\) absorb strongly; modern vapor deposition keeps) Tj
T*
(   impurities < 1 ppb.) Tj
T*
(   * Hydroxyl \(OH-\) radicals: Water vapor trapped in glass creates fundamental vibrations at 2.73) Tj
T*
ET

endstream
