(   [Problem Statement]: Compare the intermodal pulse broadening and maximum achievable transmission bit rate over a 10 km link for: \(a\) a step-index fiber with n1 = 1.50 and Delta = 0.01, and \(b\) an optimized parabolic graded-index fiber with the identical parameters.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Given Parameters:) Tj
T*
(   - Link length L = 10 km = 10,000 m; Core index n1 = 1.50; Delta = 0.01.) Tj
T*
(   - Speed of light: c = 3.0 x 10^8 m/s.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. \(a\) Step-Index \(SI\) Fiber Calculation:) Tj
T*
(   - Pulse broadening formula: Delta_t_SI = \(L * n1 * Delta\) / c.) Tj
T*
(   - Delta_t_SI = \(10,000 * 1.50 * 0.01\) / \(3.0 x 10^8\) = 150 / \(3.0 x 10^8\) = 5.0 x 10^-7 seconds) Tj
T*
(   = 500 nanoseconds.) Tj
T*
(   - Maximum theoretical bit rate B_max \(assuming B ~= 1 / \(2 * Delta_t\)\):) Tj
T*
(   B_max,SI = 1 / \(2 * 500 x 10^-9\) = 1.0 x 10^6 bps = 1.0 Mbps.) Tj
T*
(   - \(Bandwidth-distance product: B * L = 1.0 Mbps * 10 km = 10 MHz*km\).) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   3. \(b\) Parabolic Graded-Index \(GI\) Fiber Calculation:) Tj
T*
(   - Pulse broadening formula: Delta_t_GI = \(L * n1 * Delta^2\) / \(8 * c\).) Tj
T*
(   - Delta_t_GI = [ 10,000 * 1.50 * \(0.01\)^2 ] / [ 8 * \(3.0 x 10^8\) ] = \(150 * 10^-4\) / \(2.4 x) Tj
T*
(   10^9\) = 1.5 x 10^-2 / 2.4 x 10^9 = 6.25 x 10^-12 seconds = 6.25 picoseconds.) Tj
T*
(   - Maximum theoretical bit rate:) Tj
T*
(   B_max,GI = 1 / \(2 * 6.25 x 10^-12\) = 8.0 x 10^10 bps = 80 Gbps.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   4. Engineering Conclusion:) Tj
T*
(   - Graded-index profiling reduces intermodal pulse broadening by five orders of magnitude \(from) Tj
T*
(   500 ns down to 6.25 ps\), transforming a severely band-limited cable into a high-capacity) Tj
T*
(   optical channel.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
