12.5 TL
(>> Solution to Question 16:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Explain the temperature-strain cross-sensitivity problem in FBG sensors. Describe three practical optical and mechanical techniques to achieve complete temperature compensation.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. The Cross-Sensitivity Problem:) Tj
T*
(   - The total shift in reflected Bragg wavelength combines mechanical strain and thermal effects:) Tj
T*
(   Delta_lambda_B / lambda_B = S_epsilon * epsilon + S_T * Delta_T.) Tj
T*
(   - For typical silica fiber: S_epsilon ~= 0.78 x 10^-6 muepsilon^-1 \(1.2 pm/muepsilon\) and S_T) Tj
T*
(   ~= 6.8 x 10^-6 C^-1 \(10.5 pm/C\).) Tj
T*
(   - A tiny 1 C temperature fluctuation produces a 10.5 pm shift, indistinguishable from an 8.75) Tj
T*
(   microstrain mechanical load!) Tj
T*
(   - In outdoor civil infrastructure exposed to day-night temperature swings of 30 C \(~ 315 pm) Tj
T*
(   shift\), uncompensated strain readings would be completely false.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Temperature Compensation Technique 1: Dual-FBG Reference Sensor:) Tj
T*
(   - Two identical FBGs inscribed with distinct nominal wavelengths \(e.g., lambda_1 = 1540 nm,) Tj
T*
(   lambda_2 = 1545 nm\) are installed side-by-side at the same location.) Tj
T*
(   - FBG 1 is firmly bonded to the structural concrete/steel to experience both strain and) Tj
T*
(   temperature.) Tj
T*
(   - FBG 2 is placed inside a loose, sealed capillary tube attached at only one end, completely) Tj
T*
(   isolated from structural strain \(epsilon = 0\) but subjected to identical temperature.) Tj
T*
(   - Delta_lambda_2 isolates Delta_T: Delta_T = Delta_lambda_2 / S_T.) Tj
T*
(   - True strain is calculated: epsilon = \(Delta_lambda_1 - S_T*Delta_T\) / S_epsilon.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   3. Technique 2: Athermal Mechanical Packaging:) Tj
T*
(   - The FBG is pre-strained and bonded between two metal tubes with differing coefficients of) Tj
T*
(   thermal expansion \(e.g., Invar and Aluminum\).) Tj
T*
(   - Differential thermal expansion mechanically relaxes the fiber tension as temperature rises,) Tj
T*
(   perfectly counterbalancing the positive thermo-optic coefficient.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   4. Technique 3: Dual-Core / Birefringent Fiber:) Tj
T*
