/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Explain the optical construction and operation of an Extrinsic Fabry-Perot Interferometer \(EFPI\) fiber sensor. Derive its interference formula and explain its application in measuring microscopic expansion joint movements.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Construction of EFPI Sensor:) Tj
T*
(   - An Extrinsic Fabry-Perot Interferometer \(EFPI\) consists of a single-mode lead-in fiber and a) Tj
T*
(   reflecting target fiber \(or polished metal wire\) aligned inside a precision silica capillary) Tj
T*
(   tube.) Tj
T*
(   - The two cleaved, polished fiber end-faces are separated by an air gap cavity of initial) Tj
T*
(   length d \(~ 10 to 50 microns\).) Tj
T*
(   - The capillary tube is fused to the fibers at two gauge anchors spaced by distance L_g.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Optical Interference Mechanism:) Tj
T*
(   - Light traveling down the lead-in fiber hits the first silica-air boundary \(reflectivity R1 ~=) Tj
T*
(   4%\). A reference wave E1 reflects back.) Tj
T*
(   - The transmitted light traverses the air cavity, reflects off the second fiber face) Tj
T*
(   \(reflectivity R2 ~= 4%\), and re-enters the lead-in fiber as sensing wave E2.) Tj
T*
(   - The round-trip optical path difference is OPD = 2 * n_air * d = 2 * d \(since n_air ~= 1.0\).) Tj
T*
(   - The phase difference between the two reflections is:) Tj
T*
(   Delta_phi = \(2 * pi / lambda\) * OPD = \(4 * pi * d\) / lambda.) Tj
T*
(   - Interference intensity detected at the receiver:) Tj
T*
(   I\(lambda, d\) = I1 + I2 + 2 * sqrt\(I1 * I2\) * cos\( \(4 * pi * d\) / lambda \).) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET

