(   [Problem Statement]: A submarine optical telecommunication cable deployed across a deep river estuary experiences water ingress and cable stretching. Describe how OTDR and BOTDA can pinpoint the exact fault location.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Nature of Estuary Cable Faults:) Tj
T*
(   - Submarine cables are vulnerable to ship anchor dragging, underwater dredging, and hydraulic) Tj
T*
(   sediment scour.) Tj
T*
(   - Fault manifestations: Mechanical fiber tension \(strain\), microbending from armor crushing,) Tj
T*
(   and water ingress causing severe hydrogen darkening / localized attenuation.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Fault Pinpointing via OTDR:) Tj
T*
(   - An OTDR instrument at the shore station launches nanosecond optical pulses.) Tj
T*
(   - If the fiber is crushed or broken, a sharp Fresnel reflection spike occurs followed by) Tj
T*
(   complete signal drop-off.) Tj
T*
(   - Distance to break: z_fault = c * t_roundtrip / \(2 * n_g\).) Tj
T*
(   - If water ingress causes localized microbending without breakage, a sharp step-drop \(e.g., 2.5) Tj
T*
(   dB localized drop\) appears on the backscatter curve, locating water infiltration within +- 0.5) Tj
T*
(   m.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   3. Structural Assessment via BOTDA:) Tj
T*
(   - BOTDA measures the continuous strain profile along the cable.) Tj
T*
(   - If anchor snagging has stretched a 50-meter section to 3000 microstrain, the Brillouin) Tj
T*
(   frequency shifts upward by Delta_nu_B = 150 MHz over that specific zone.) Tj
T*
(   - Provides early warning of imminent mechanical failure long before complete fiber breakage) Tj
T*
(   occurs, allowing preventative marine diver intervention.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
