(   [Problem Statement]: Discuss Polarization Mode Dispersion \(PMD\) in single-mode optical fibers. Explain its origin from core asymmetry and external structural stress, and state its impact on high-speed civil telemetry links.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Origin of PMD:) Tj
T*
(   - In ideal theoretical single-mode fiber, the core is perfectly circular and isotropic,) Tj
T*
(   supporting two degenerate orthogonal polarization modes \(LP01x and LP01y\) propagating at) Tj
T*
(   identical velocities.) Tj
T*
(   - In practical real-world fibers, structural imperfections break this cylindrical symmetry:) Tj
T*
(   * Intrinsic factors: Geometric core ellipticity and non-uniform thermal stress frozen during) Tj
T*
(   drawing.) Tj
T*
(   * Extrinsic civil factors: Lateral cable clamping pressure, sharp bends inside conduits, and) Tj
T*
(   ground settlement squeezing the cable.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Modal Birefringence:) Tj
T*
(   - These asymmetric stresses induce optical birefringence: B = |n_x - n_y| != 0.) Tj
T*
(   - The two orthogonal polarization states travel at different group velocities \(fast axis and) Tj
T*
(   slow axis\).) Tj
T*
(   - Differential Group Delay \(DGD\) Delta_tau develops between polarizations.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   3. Random Polarization Mode Coupling and Square-Root Length Law:) Tj
T*
(   - Because stress perturbations fluctuate randomly along kilometer spans, continuous random mode) Tj
T*
(   coupling occurs between polarizations.) Tj
T*
(   - Consequently, average pulse spreading scales with the square root of link length L:) Tj
T*
(   <Delta_tau> = D_PMD * sqrt\(L\),) Tj
T*
(   where D_PMD is the PMD parameter \(typically 0.05 to 0.5 ps / sqrt\(km\)\).) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   4. Telemetry Impact:) Tj
T*
(   - Unlike chromatic dispersion, PMD cannot be compensated by passive optical gratings because it) Tj
T*
(   fluctuates dynamically with ambient temperature and wind sway on bridges.) Tj
T*
(   - Sets the ultimate physical transmission distance barrier for 40 Gbps and 100 Gbps regional) Tj
T*
(   telemetry networks.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F2 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(>> Solution to Question 27:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Explain the optical characteristics and advantages of Polymer Optical Fibers \(POF\) compared to silica glass fibers. Discuss their niche applications in civil building networks and large-strain crack monitoring.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Material and Physical Structure of POF:) Tj
T*
(   - Core: Polymethyl methacrylate \(PMMA, optical acrylic\) with refractive index n1 ~= 1.492.) Tj
T*
(   - Cladding: Fluorinated polymer with index n2 ~= 1.402 to 1.417.) Tj
T*
(   - Enormous Dimensions: Core diameter typically 980 micrometers, outer cladding 1000 micrometers) Tj
T*
(   \(1.0 mm!\), compared to 9 um for silica SMF.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Optical and Mechanical Advantages:) Tj
T*
(   - \(a\) Massive Numerical Aperture \(NA ~= 0.50\): Huge acceptance angle \(theta_a ~= 30 degrees\)) Tj
T*
(   allows simple light coupling from inexpensive visible red LEDs \(650 nm\).) Tj
T*
(   - \(b\) High Mechanical Ductility and Flexibility: Can withstand tight bend radii \(< 5 mm\)) Tj
T*
(   without cracking; failure strain exceeds 10% to 50% elongation \(compared to < 1% for brittle) Tj
T*
(   silica glass\).) Tj
T*
(   - \(c\) Do-It-Yourself Splicing: Cuts cleanly with a simple razor blade and connects without) Tj
T*
(   expensive fusion splicers.) Tj
T*
ET

